kind: llman.sdd.delta
ops[4]{op,req_id,title,statement,from,to,name}:
  modify_requirement,tui50,"streaming-mutable-region-rendered","The TUI streaming render MUST grow the in-progress (not-yet-newline-terminated) content as rendered lines at the top of the tail region (visually continuous with the scrollback above), rendered within the ratatui buffer (inline viewport), NOT via direct terminal escape-sequence writes that bypass the ratatui buffer. The mutable region holds the rendered lines of the accumulated turn source beyond the committed boundary, re-rendered in full each frame from the complete raw source so cross-paragraph context (block blank-line spacing, list/blockquote prefix continuity) is preserved. Complete newline-terminated stable lines MUST be committed incrementally to the scrollback via insert_before as soon as their newline boundary arrives. The tail region MUST contain only: the mutable rendered lines (top, redrawn each frame, transparent background), the thinking/status indicator, and the input prompt (bottom). The mutable lines MUST wrap to multiple rows when they exceed the terminal width (honoring CJK display width); rows beyond the tail capacity are dropped from the top (content is not lost — it commits to scrollback on the next newline). TurnEnd MUST flush any residual un-terminated rendered lines to the scrollback. The core mechanism is an append-only raw source buffer plus a full re-render on each delta, committing only the delta of newly-stable rendered lines, modeled on codex StreamCore in simplified form (no commit-tick animation, no finalize canonicalize, no stable-region replacement).",null,null,null
  modify_requirement,tui75,"stream-incremental-full-source-render","The TUI MUST render streaming assistant text incrementally with full-source rendering granularity: each TextDelta appends to an append-only raw source buffer, the COMPLETE accumulated source is re-rendered via render_markdown on every delta (so the renderer always sees the whole document and cross-paragraph context is preserved), and the delta of newly-stable rendered lines (up to the last newline boundary) is committed to scrollback WITH syntax highlighting. The unstable tail (rendered lines beyond the committed boundary) stays in the mutable region, also highlighted. This gives streaming-time line-by-line scrollback updates with full markdown/highlight context, and the streaming output MUST equal a one-shot full render of the complete source (the streaming-equals-full invariant). Code blocks render highlighted even with unclosed fences.",null,null,null
  add_requirement,tui80,"render-granularity-is-full-message","The TUI streaming render granularity MUST be the complete accumulated turn source (the entire raw markdown received so far in the turn), NOT the individual paragraph or line being committed. Each TextDelta triggers a full re-render of the accumulated source; only the resulting delta of new rendered lines (between the previously-committed boundary and the current stable boundary) is committed to the scrollback. This decouples render granularity (full message, so the renderer sees cross-paragraph structure) from commit granularity (line, so streaming still updates scrollback promptly). Evidence: codex StreamCore stores raw_source and recompute_streaming_render rebuilds rendered_lines from the full source on every delta (codex-rs/tui/src/streaming/controller.rs:76,292-294); the prior xylitol model rendered each committed paragraph independently, losing cross-paragraph context and forcing unconditional blank-line hacks.",null,null,null
  add_requirement,tui81,"streaming-equals-full-render-invariant","The TUI streaming pipeline MUST satisfy the invariant that committing a markdown source one delta at a time produces the same final scrollback line sequence as rendering the complete source in one shot. This MUST be verified by a test that feeds the same markdown through both paths (streamed char-by-char with incremental commits, and one-shot full render) and asserts the committed line sequences are equal in text and styling. Evidence: codex pins this invariant with controller_loose_vs_tight_with_commit_ticks_matches_full (codex-rs/tui/src/streaming/controller.rs:1085-1200); the prior paragraph-split model could not satisfy it because each paragraph was an independent render_markdown call with no cross-paragraph context.",null,null,null
op_scenarios[6]{req_id,id,given,when,then}:
  tui50,"mutable-region-holds-rendered-lines","streaming text is in progress with an unterminated tail spanning multiple rendered lines","the mutable region is rendered each frame","the mutable region shows the full-source-rendered lines beyond the committed boundary (highlighted, not plain text) and is visually continuous with the scrollback above"
  tui75,"full-source-render-preserves-context","an assistant reply containing a paragraph followed by a code block is streamed delta by delta",each delta triggers a full re-render of the accumulated source,"the blank-line spacing between the paragraph and the code block renders correctly without an unconditional blank-line hack (the renderer saw the full document)"
  tui80,"delta-commits-new-stable-lines-only","a TextDelta arrives that crosses a newline boundary in the accumulated source","the full re-render runs and the stable boundary is computed","only the rendered lines between the previous committed boundary and the new stable boundary are committed to scrollback (no re-commit, no dropped lines)"
  tui80,"commit-granularity-stays-line","a streaming turn produces many new stable rendered lines from one delta","the lines are committed via insert_before","scrollback updates promptly per line (commit granularity remains line-level even though render granularity is full-message)"
  tui81,"streamed-equals-full-text","a markdown source with headings, bold, a list, a blockquote, and a fenced code block is fed to the StreamBuffer one character at a time and also rendered in one shot","the final committed line sequences are compared","the streamed sequence text equals the one-shot sequence text on every line"
  tui81,"streamed-equals-full-styling","the same streamed vs one-shot comparison is made",the line styling (span styles) are compared,"the streamed sequence styling equals the one-shot sequence styling on every line"
